Two-file fix, no call-site churn, no DI plumbing:

1. **AutoReconnect.cs** — inside both WithRetry (line ~71) and WithRetrySync (line ~111), wrap the `log?.LogWarning` block with an unconditional WireLog.RecordSync emission so retries surface in the same lacerte-YYYYMMDD.log stream as the wrapped op:
   ```csharp
   var delay = BackoffMs[attempt];
   WireLog.RecordSync<bool>("AutoReconnect.retry",
       new { attempt = attempt + 1, max = MaxRetries + 1, hresult = ex.HResult.ToString("X8"), exType = ex.GetType().Name, delayMs = delay },
       () => true);
   log?.LogWarning(ex, "Transient COM/RPC failure on attempt {Attempt}/{Max}; retrying in {DelayMs}ms.", attempt + 1, MaxRetries + 1, delay);
   await Task.Delay(delay).ConfigureAwait(false);
   ```
   And at each rethrow site (lines 81-86 and 119-124), add one more:
   ```csharp
   WireLog.RecordSync<bool>("AutoReconnect.exhausted",
       new { attempts = MaxRetries + 1, hresult = last.HResult.ToString("X8"), exType = last.GetType().Name },
       () => true);
   ```
   WireLog.RecordSync is a near-zero-cost passthrough when LACERTE_DEBUG_WIRE=0 (one Lazy<T> read + null check per WireLog.cs:183-184), so the production fast-path stays free. Keep the `ILogger? log` parameter for future-proofing but stop pretending the existing call sites pass one.

